I won’t need — and it’s safer not to share — your SSN for this. I also want to check: when you say “expiry date,” do you mean your passport’s expiration? (That’s what’s usually relevant for visas.) If so, here’s how to check whether it’s “too close” for a six‑month visa.

Quick rules of thumb
- Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your intended date of entry or beyond your intended date of departure. Requirements vary by country/visa type.  
- If your passport will expire within six months of the end of your planned stay (or six months after entry, per the embassy’s rule), you will very likely need to renew it before travel or before applying for the visa.

How to check step‑by‑step
1. Find your passport expiry date (month/year).  
2. Determine the visa dates: either the visa validity or your intended travel/return dates.  
