Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Quick privacy note: please avoid sharing your full SSN in chat or on forms unless absolutely necessary. (SSNs themselves do not expire.)

Do you mean your passport expiry date? Visa/entry rules generally refer to passport validity, not SSNs. Here’s the typical rule and what to do:

- Typical requirement: many countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond your intended date of entry or beyond your planned departure date. Some require six months beyond the visa end date.
- How to check for your case: compare your passport expiry date to the visa start/end or your travel dates. If your passport expires less than six months after those dates, you should renew before applying for the visa or traveling.
- Practical advice: if expiry < (visa start date + 6 months) or < (planned return date + 6 months) — renew now. Also factor in passport renewal processing time and any appointment delays.
- Exceptions: some countries or visa types have different rules; consulate websites or the destination country’s entry rules are authoritative.

If you like, tell me:
- the passport expiry date,
- the visa start date or intended travel dates, and
- the destination country

I can check the specific requirement for that country and tell you whether you need to renew, and help draft any emails or renewal forms.

