I won’t forward or store your SSN. I can help you with what to do, but I won’t send that personal info anywhere.

Quick answer (rule of thumb)
- Most countries require your passport to be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned departure/period of stay. So if you plan to use the full six-month visa, your passport should typically be valid at least 6 months after the visa end date.
- If your passport expires during the visa period (or sooner than that 6‑month buffer), you’ll likely need to renew the passport before traveling or risk being denied boarding/entry.

Things to check right now
1. Destination rules — check the embassy/consulate website for that country’s passport-validity requirement (some countries have exceptions).  
2. Airline policy — airlines will often refuse boarding if passport validity is insufficient even if an embassy would let you enter.  
3. Visa vs passport relationship — for many countries a visa in an expired passport can still be usable if you carry both the old passport (with visa) and a new passport, but policies differ and it can cause delays at immigration.  
4. If you must renew — research normal vs expedited passport renewal timelines and fees.

Safety note about the form asking for SSN
- Only provide your SSN on secure, legitimate requests. Verify the organization, confirm the site is HTTPS, and ask why they need the SSN and whether you can provide just the last 4 digits instead. Don’t email your full SSN or paste it into messages unless you’re sure the recipient is authorized and the channel is secure.

If you want, I can:
- Look up the passport-validity/entry rules for the specific destination (tell me the country and your passport expiry date and your planned travel or visa start date), or
- Draft a secure message to the agency/embassy asking whether your passport expiry is acceptable, or
- Help you fill the form while omitting sensitive fields (and advise what to put).

Which would you like me to do next?

